{:edb/db-before-t 818 :edb/db-after-t 819 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534131 50 #inst "2026-10-06T04:06:54.795Z" 13194139534131 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278245 13194139534131 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278226 13194139534131 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278233 13194139534131 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278238 13194139534131 true] [17592186092821 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534131 true] [17592186092821 1100 "Find the solution(s) to the equation $2\\cos x + 2 = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$." 13194139534131 false] [17592186092821 1100 "Find the solution(s) to the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$" 13194139534131 true] [17592186092821 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\pi $ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\pi .$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx=\\pi +2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is the only solution." 13194139534131 true] [17592186278226 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278227 13194139534131 true] [17592186278226 1089 "q-120989" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278226 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534131 true] [17592186278226 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.9$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278226 1113 #uuid "c7c27ee1-3dad-5834-b592-aad9797bac23" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278226 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.9)=25.841\\,932{…}^{∘}≈{25.84}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${25.84}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/4c65ecb4fdcefc332b75f2b459f65ba872a2d2d0826b27ba80df1c8f8f3b606e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{25.84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={334.16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={334.16}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{25.84}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=25.84\\cdot (-25.84)≈-670\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten." 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278228 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278229 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278230 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278231 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278232 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1041 "selection" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278229 13194139534131 true] [17592186278227 1110 #uuid "441dceb6-7ee1-54a3-b35a-6ef51a6df2e3" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278228 1034 "-610" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278228 1111 #uuid "89356a7b-59fa-5f75-96c2-e2cbd1787120" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278229 1034 "-670" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278229 1111 #uuid "a7c5042b-37f9-5757-b786-97efa1e97499" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278230 1034 "-560" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278230 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278230 1111 #uuid "07f2daad-1f76-5441-b88b-f86f790f010e" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278231 1034 "-650" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278231 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278231 1111 #uuid "aa0dc536-a707-54b6-923b-b6584bf5ea7e" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278232 1034 "-590" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278232 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278232 1111 #uuid "b89f984c-b009-5a3a-9a8f-e7b38e7cbfc3" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278234 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278236 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1089 "q-338573" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1100 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1113 #uuid "bd69c5c8-719b-532b-8d1c-6d7b04b275f1" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278233 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/91e4de557177693203cbbc48c12bbf7af3de1739c8405ac05bdfa06d3e92638c.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-135}^{∘}$ and $x={135}^{∘}.$" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278234 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278235 13194139534131 true] [17592186278234 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534131 true] [17592186278234 1041 "field-1" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278234 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278235 13194139534131 true] [17592186278234 1110 #uuid "519ecf6b-bba2-55bf-9017-4fb1b4847e58" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278235 1034 "-135" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278235 1111 #uuid "828955d0-4bdc-5c49-b12f-6ce3cc3d47db" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278236 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278237 13194139534131 true] [17592186278236 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534131 true] [17592186278236 1041 "field-2" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278236 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278237 13194139534131 true] [17592186278236 1110 #uuid "621ad334-5a88-5156-8f3d-75e59356fa70" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278237 1034 "135" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278237 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278237 1111 #uuid "96767617-dbb2-5ccc-bfcb-1e0de7d8bcfe" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278239 13194139534131 true] [17592186278238 1089 "q-72841" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534131 true] [17592186278238 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278238 1113 #uuid "39ec17cf-e2ec-5e6d-90c9-4664e7af0135" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278238 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{5}) & =1.982\\,313…≈1.982\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.982\\,3$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.982\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.982\\,3 & ≈1.159\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.159\\,3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/250adbaae0378e3f42ab4340479bb4590aa7f39a1094ea39010de3d5758b4e3e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.159\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.300\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.982\\,3\\cdot 4.300\\,9≈8.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278240 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278241 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278242 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278243 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278244 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1041 "selection" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278244 13194139534131 true] [17592186278239 1110 #uuid "3c76c249-52f5-53a8-8e1c-50bdf5723b6c" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278240 1034 "8.9" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278240 1111 #uuid "78fa451f-c205-501a-8823-be130e94e18b" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278241 1034 "6.3" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278241 1111 #uuid "3ca356de-e7d0-5f99-9feb-5d5abb3ca6c8" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278242 1034 "7.5" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278242 1111 #uuid "ee63ea55-8b55-5815-9c45-c118b4c974f0" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278243 1034 "9.2" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278243 1111 #uuid "8ee0c786-f018-5427-a547-7884dac6cef8" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278244 1034 "8.5" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278244 1111 #uuid "ba6741f0-d8a6-59a4-99f5-49c004acd85e" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278245 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278246 13194139534131 true] [17592186278245 1089 "q-72845" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278245 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534131 true] [17592186278245 1100 "Find the solution(s) to the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2}=1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278245 1113 #uuid "c6affe1a-b4aa-5c33-add9-f7ef175f8a68" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278245 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278247 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278248 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278249 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278250 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278251 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1041 "selection" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278248 13194139534131 true] [17592186278246 1110 #uuid "2abec0f8-c293-5a1e-b660-9e22a95b2bab" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278247 1034 "x={30}^{∘},\\,{210}^{∘}" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534131 true] [17592186278247 1111 #uuid "5eee05a2-f6d2-5b71-b692-cacea99c0406" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278248 1034 "x={180}^{∘}" 13194139534131 true] [17592186278248 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131941395341